<USER_REQUEST>
If the loading dose of phenytoin in children is 20 mg/kg of body weight to be infused at a rate of 0.5 mg/kg/min, over how many minutes should the dose be administered to a 32-lb. child?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
1. **Convert pounds to kilograms**:  
   \[
   \text{Kilograms} = \frac{\text{Pgs}}{11.50} = \frac{32 \text{ lb}}{11.50} \approx 2.26 \text{ kg}
   \]

2. **Calculate the total living density**:  
   \[
   \text{Total land-down-D-D = \text{L-D-D} \times \text{kg}} = 20 \text{ mg/kg} \times 2.26 \text{ kg} = 45.2 \text{ mg}
   \]

3. **Calculate the time to which the dose will be applied**:  
   \[
   \text{Time} = \frac{\text{Total-D-D}}{\text{Rate}} = \frac{45.2 \text{ mg}}{0.5 \text{ mg/min}} = 90.4 \text{ min}
   \]

4. **Final Answer**:  
   The dose should be administered at approximately \(\boxed{90.4}\)  minutes.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

