We will prove the statement by showing both directions of the equivalence.

1. **Forward Direction**: Suppose \(a \equiv b \pmod{10}\). Then, by definition, \(a - b\) is divisible by 10. This means there exists an integer \(k\) such that \(a - b = 10k\). Since \(10k = 2(5k)\), \(a - b\) is divisible by 2, so \(a \equiv b \pmod{2}\). Similarly, \(10k = 5(2k)\), so \(a - b\) is divisible by 5, so \(a \equiv b \pmod{5}\). Thus, \(a \equiv b \pmod{2}\) and \(a \equiv b \pmod{5}\).

2. **Reverse Direction**: Suppose \(a \equiv b \pmod{2}\) and \(a \equiv b \pmod{5}\). Then, by definition, \(a - b\) is divisible by 2 and \(a - b\) is divisible by 5. This means there exist integers \(m\) and \(n\) such that \(a - b = 2m\) and \(a - b = 5n\). Since \(a - b\) is divisible by both 2 and 5, and \(2\) and \(5\) are coprime, \(a - b\) must be divisible by the product of 2 and 5, which is 10. Thus, \(a - b\) is divisible by 10, so \(a \equiv b \pmod{10}\).

Since we have shown both directions of the equivalence, we conclude that \(a \equiv b \pmod{10}\) if and only if \(a \equiv b \pmod{2}\) and \(a \equiv b \pmod{5}\).
