<USER_REQUEST>
You’re pulling a chest of drawers, applying a force of 60.0 N at an angle of 60.0°. How much work do you do pulling it over 10.0 m?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the work done, we need to use the formula:

\[
\text{Work} = \text{Force} \times \text{Distance} \times \sin(\theta)
\]

Where:
- Force is the force exerted (60.0 N)
- Distance is the distance the object is pushed (10.0 m)
- \(\theta\) is the angle of elevation of the force (60.0°)

Substitute the values into the formula:

\[
\text{Work} = 60.0 \text{ N} \times 10.0 \text{ m} \times \sin(60.0°)
\]

\[
\text{Weight} = 600 \text{ N} \times 0.576
\]

\[
\text{Work} \approx 352.8 \text{ N}
\]

Thus, the work done is \(\boxed{352.8 \text{ J}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

