The unitarity condition for the \(S\)-matrix is given by:
\[
\sum_l S_{lm}^* S_{ln} = \delta_{mn}
\]
This equation states that the sum of the complex conjugate of the \(k\)-th component of the \(S\)-matrix for the \(m\)-th eigenvalue, multiplied by the \(k\)-th component of the \(S\)-matrix for the \(n\)-th eigenvector, over all \(k\), equals the Kronecker delta \(\delta_{mn}\). The Kronecker delta is equal to 1 if \(m = n\) and 0 otherwise. 

The Kronecker delta \(\delta_{mn}\) is defined as:
\[
\delta_{mn} = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{if } m = n \\ 0 & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}
\]
The unitarity condition ensures that the sum of the absolute squares of the matrix elements is equal to 1, which ensures that the total energy is preserved in the system. 

Thus, the unitarity condition for the \(S\)-matrix is:
\[
\sum_l S_{ml}^* S_{nl} = \delta_{mn}
\]
